Here is my understanding.

**The standard.** Every component of what Jev reads and of the dispute model says only what that thing is. A rule states the rule, the way the statute or the contract states it. A decision states who decides what. A record item names the record. A parameter states its value and where the value comes from. A timing line states when the thing happens.

**What that excludes.** Anything else in a component is a defect, whether or not it matches a pattern I already know:
- how I organized the build (register codes, status words, approval stamps);
- what it used to be, or what it is not (corrections, contrasts, "never", "rather than", "not from");
- who reviewed it or why it changed;
- guardrails aimed at my own past mistakes;
- our modelling constructs where the world has its own terms ("tau", "horizon", "feasibility bound", "path");
- explanations of the machinery addressed to whoever reads the component;
- hedges and caveats.

**The method.** No search list. A grep for "L11" or "never" only finds what I already suspect, and you've said we don't know everything that's wrong. So I go through every component one at a time and ask of each: what is this thing, and does the text say exactly that and nothing else?

- **What Jev reads**, recursively, in the form Jev actually receives it:
  - the case header;
  - each question's wording, answer options and instructions;
  - each legal standard as passed;
  - record items, assumptions and path facts, as the code renders them;
  - the evidence and readings wrapping.
- **The dispute model**, top to bottom: its description, every rule, parameter, template, node and field, and the timing and effect texts. That includes the parts Jev never sees, because the model is the model regardless of who reads it.

**Two consequences:**
- **Code that reads a component's text** gets its references updated in the same edit, so the model and the code stay aligned.
- **Jev's question wording will change,** so its forecasts are asked again (a few cents) and the analysis is rerun. Numbers may move slightly. I'll report any that do, and I won't adjust anything to keep them where they were.

**The deliverable.** Every changed component shown as before → after, grouped by where it sits, plus the rerun's results.

I'll start on your GO.

