I rewrote PR #17's title and description to state what the PR is. The new title is "Recorded Akoustis run at 20 Jun 2024, with each Jev question asked in its situation on its record items": https://github.com/owassmer/Slope_Sparse_Events/pull/17

The description now opens with the run and its numbers. It then lists what is in the model and in Jev's inputs, in plain terms. The final-judgment rule is one entry among the model's rules. It no longer shares the title with the recorded run.

Two early commits still carry the old wording ("L11 settled", "Owen, 26 Sep 2026"). Removing them from history would mean a force-push, so instead the description recommends a squash merge, which uses the description as the single commit message.

The PR has no merge-ready label. Its content has changed substantially since the last review, so it needs one focused review first; I can dispatch it now.

**Post-demo items**

**1. The agent attaches its findings to the record items as it investigates**

*What it is.* The design (spec §3.5) makes finding record items the agent's job, because it is the one that read the documents. The agent would be told each question's record items, grouped by matter:

- the court's rulings;
- stays and early registration;
- Qorvo's choices;
- Akoustis's choices;
- the notes;
- the listing.

When it groups the dispute and the notes, it attaches each accepted finding to the items it supplies, and code checks the link. An item it searched for and didn't find is recorded as searched and absent. It also records the case facts the arithmetic needs but it missed this time: when the case began, and the sealed interest motion (D.I. 615).

*Scope:*

- the agent's instructions;
- one argument each on the two grouping tools, with code checks;
- routing reads the agent's links, and the Jev matching screen is removed;
- a new recorded investigation, which replaces the current run, then a re-analysis.

*How it improves the build:*

- **20 record items stop being missing without anyone having looked.** The agent goes looking for each one. The ones that matter most:
  - the damages exhibits (D.I. 616), so the damages ruling isn't judged on Akoustis's motion alone;
  - the 10-Q's note on where Akoustis's assets sit, which is what early registration turns on;
  - the verdict form, for trebling and interest;
  - any proxy or vote history, for the reverse split.
- **Pre-judgment interest becomes real.** With the start date recorded, the statute computes it: about $6.58M on the full verdict. The court's interest question gets asked, and the interest motion delays the appeal deadline as the design says. The judgment amounts on every path become correct.
- **Jev's matching errors disappear.** The matching is done by the reader of the documents, and code checks each link.

**2. Dated contract triggers in each question's facts**

*What it is.* Spec §3.3 lists the dated contract triggers among the facts every question receives. Code already computes these dates on each path:

- the 15 Dec coupon;
- the 21 Oct Nasdaq deadline and that path's delisting date;
- the repurchase due date;
- the day the judgment default ripens (60 days after the ruling);
- the appeal deadline.

They just aren't passed to Jev.

*Scope:* one addition to the path facts, taken from each path's trace, plus the model's text.

*How it improves the build:* decisions are driven by what is about to fall due, and Jev can't see it today. Some examples:

- Akoustis at its cash floor in November doesn't know the notes default ripens in a few weeks.
- A settlement question doesn't see that clock either, though it is Qorvo's and the noteholders' main leverage.
- The noteholders deciding on the judgment default don't know when the stock faces delisting.

**3. The injunction's stress scenario**

*What it is.* The design (DECOMPOSITION §5.3) sets this out. If the court enters the trade-secret injunction, a worldwide ban on products made with Qorvo's trade secrets, RF Filters receipts fall to zero from the order date. That segment is about 63% of revenue and is the only bound the record gives. The scenario is shown as a stress view, never weighted into the forecast, with the injunction question asked again so its probability sits beside it.

*Scope:* one switch in the engine that cuts that share of receipts from the order date, one row in the stress view, and the injunction question asked again.

*How it improves the build:* today the most severe operational risk in the case has no effect anywhere, and the page can't show it. With the stress view, Russell sees what Slope's collections look like if most revenue stops. It is labelled as a bound from the record, not a forecast.

**4. Clawback exposure net of new value**

*What it is.* Payments Slope collects in the 90 days before a filing can be clawed back (§547). A creditor that extends new credit after being paid can offset it (§547(c)(4)), and Slope keeps funding invoices on a revolving line. The design (DECOMPOSITION §1.3) reports exposure both gross and net of those later draws.

*Scope:* arithmetic over each trajectory's collections and fundings in the 90-day window, one output, and one figure on the page.

*How it improves the build:* the $148k shown today is gross, so it overstates what Slope actually has at risk. The net figure is the one Slope's counsel would argue. It is also a direct lender number, and it shows why a revolving line behaves differently in bankruptcy from a single loan.

**Order after the demo:** 1 first, because it changes what every question reads and it fixes the interest amount; then 2, which is small; then 4, then 3.

I've sent PR #17 to three independent reviewers, running in parallel. Each will post one comment on the PR. When they finish, I'll bring you all their findings with my ruling on each.

1. **Law and decision structure.** Read as a litigator, a restructuring lawyer and a decision analyst would. It checks:
   - that each rule matches its source;
   - that each question is one actor's decision;
   - that the branches and situations match how these actors actually decide.
2. **Probability and cash arithmetic.** Read as a quant, a credit analyst and a code reviewer would. It checks:
   - that the new branches still add up to one;
   - that each branch books exactly the cash and dates it should;
   - that the outcome shares reconcile with the bankruptcy probability;
   - that no unknown silently becomes zero and no fact from after 20 June leaks in.
3. **Jev's inputs and the evidence chain.** Read as an LLM-evaluation researcher, a forecasting practitioner and the lender. It rebuilds exactly what Jev received for every question and checks:
   - that each question has what its decision needs, from both sides;
   - that the record-item matches are right;
   - that nothing frames Jev toward an answer;
   - that the page lets Russell see where the record ends and judgment begins.

All three were told:

- the "say only what it is" standard and the design-to-code rule;
- the deferred items, so they don't report those as findings;
- to mark each finding blocking or a note, and report only what breaks the chain from evidence to the lender's cash;
- not to call Jev or edit anything.
